internal/controlplane/store/project_quota_concurrent_test.go:66:		`INSERT INTO quota_policies (id, scope_kind, plan, resource, limit_value, enforcement_mode)
ralph/progress.txt:74:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **TENANT-SCOPED EVENT-LIKE LEDGER table whose ONLY public mutation surface is an unbounded INSERT (no UNIQUE on (organization_id, resource) — only PRIMARY KEY (id) and a composite-FK target UNIQUE (organization_id, id)), whose ONLY read aggregate is a SUM filtered by tenant + resource + status + expires_at predicates, and whose row body carries nullable timestamp/text columns** (BE-0456 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_reservation_tenant_isolation_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0454 (quota_usage — partial UNIQUE (organization_id, resource) and the ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING / SELECT FOR UPDATE primitive) in three load-bearing ways: (a) the bystander byte-identity assertion must cover the nullable job_id and settled_at columns (rawQuotaReservationRow declares them *string/*time.Time; the per-file stringPtrEqualQR/timePtrEqualQR/fmtStringPtrQR/fmtTimePtrQR helpers MUST get a per-file QR suffix because store_test already exposes QP-suffixed variants from BE-0451 and a stringPtrEqual variant from BE-0440); (b) the same-resource-in-two-tenants positive-direction pair is INSERT-and-both-persist (no conflict at all — no UNIQUE constraint to defeat) instead of LockUsage-and-both-succeed; (c) a NEW cross-tenant probe is mandatory — global PRIMARY KEY collision across tenants must surface as typed apierr.Conflict (yerr.CodeConflict) AND leave the prior owner's row byte-identical AND insert no row for the requester; this guards against a regression that swapped the INSERT for an ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE (a copy-paste of quota_policies' upsert pattern). The 7-test shape (insert-bystander-byte-identity across active+settled peers / insert-row-count-invariant / same-resource-two-tenants-both-persist / global-PK-collision-cross-tenant-conflict with byte-identical prior-owner / SumActiveReservations-tenant-scoped (10-vs-300 chosen so a cross-tenant fold shows as 310) / SumActiveReservations-on-empty-tenant-returns-zero-even-when-others-have-rows / organization-delete-cascade-is-tenant-scoped) leaves the BE-0455 invariants tests (status / time / resource predicate filters, CHECK / DOMAIN / FK violations, row-shape, id-provenance, rollback, nil-tx) untouched. Helpers reused: seedOrg, insertReservationRow, insertSettledReservationRow, loadQuotaReservationRowByID, countQuotaReservationRowsForOrg, runInsertReservationOrFail, newStore. New helpers introduced (unique to this file): assertQuotaReservationByteIdentical (anchored on updated_at via the BEFORE UPDATE quota_reservations_set_updated_at trigger).
ralph/progress.txt:75:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **TENANT-SCOPED COUNTER table whose ONLY public mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT (organization_id, resource) DO NOTHING + SELECT...FOR UPDATE primitive (LockUsage), with NO per-row Update/Delete, NO version column, NO service_id pointer, and a database-managed used_value mutated by an EXTERNAL code path (quota.Checker, not the repository), set_updated_at trigger fires BEFORE UPDATE only** (BE-0453 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_usage_repository_invariants_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0451 (quota_policies — INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert) in four load-bearing ways: (a) the second-call byte-identity proof must anchor updated_at TOO — ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING is not an UPDATE and the set_updated_at trigger must not fire (a regression that switched to ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE with even a no-op SET would silently bump updated_at and a missing anchor would let that slip past); (b) the peer-row proof is a SAME-TENANT-DIFFERENT-RESOURCE check (UNIQUE (organization_id, resource) isolates rows by resource within a tenant); (c) the row-already-exists-with-non-zero-value leg has no policies analog — pre-seed via raw SQL at a sentinel value (e.g. used_value=17, id=`qu_seed_<resource>`) and prove BOTH the seeded id AND the seeded used_value survive a LockUsage call (this is the ONLY way to prove the discarded INSERT's minted prefix id does not replace the row's id — a regression to ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE SET id=EXCLUDED.id would be a catastrophic data-loss path that typed reader projections would not catch); (d) the DOMAIN-violation test casts an arbitrary string to the typed Resource (e.g. `store.QuotaResource("not-a-resource")`) to bypass Go's compile-time check and exercise the DOMAIN quota_resource CHECK at the database layer — surfacing as yerr.CodeConflict through mapWriteError. The 8-new-test shape (row-shape, second-call-byte-identity, peer-row-byte-identity, persisted-used-value, unknown-org-Conflict, unknown-resource-Conflict, tx-rollback, nil-tx-Internal) leaves the pre-existing TestQuotaRepositoryLockUsageCreatesAndReturnsCounter in quota_repository_test.go alone as the load-bearing two-call-idempotency probe. Helper symbols introduced: `lockUsageOrFail`, `loadQuotaUsageRow`, `countQuotaUsageRowsForOrg`, `rawQuotaUsageRow`, `quotaUsageTxRollbackSentinel`. Reused symbols from sibling files: `seedOrg` (schema_test.go), `seedQuotaUsage` (usage_test.go), `newStore` (store_test.go) — note `seedQuotaUsage` is the ONLY way to set used_value to a non-zero value through a test fixture because the repository surface has no equivalent (LockUsage cannot increment).
ralph/progress.txt:76:- Repository tenant-isolation tests for a **DUAL-SCOPE ledger-style table whose ONLY mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert and whose row body is observable EXCLUSIVELY through typed resolvers (no per-id Get/Update/Delete, no ListByParent)** (BE-0452 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_policy_tenant_isolation_test.go). quota_policies has TWO scope_kind values ('plan_default' with NULL organization_id; 'organization' with non-NULL) anchored by partial unique indexes (e.g. `quota_policies_org_resource_idx (organization_id, resource) WHERE scope_kind='organization'`) and NO version column — so the byte-identical-bystander anchor reduces to the trigger-managed updated_at ALONE. The 9-test shape diverges from BE-0440 (project_grant, ListByParent-only) and BE-0436 (api_key_scope, per-id Get + per-id mutations) in four load-bearing ways: (a) cross-tenant projection is proved through EVERY resolver leg independently — one test per resolver method (EffectiveLimit per-resource tenant-scoped; ListEffectiveLimits count isolation; ListOrganizationUsage two-CTE-leg isolation) — because each resolver has its own WHERE clause and a regression dropping any one leg leaks a different column; (b) a NEW plan-isolation leg is added: orgA on 'starter' must not see 'pro' plan defaults even though both rows live in the SAME table with NULL organization_id — the plan column is the second tenant boundary on the plan_default scope and a missing plan predicate is a distinct regression surface from a missing organization_id predicate; (c) the byte-identical-bystander proof is observed via a RAW-SQL load of the underlying quota_policies row (reusing BE-0451's loadOrganizationPolicyRow + rawQuotaPolicyRow), because the typed reader projections deliberately omit id/created_at/updated_at/scope_kind/plan — a regression that stamped correct-looking limit_value and enforcement_mode onto the peer's row would slip past every typed-resolver projection if asserted only via EffectiveLimit; (d) ListOrganizationUsage has TWO independent tenant-predicate legs (one in the policies CTE on organization_id, one in the usage CTE on organization_id) — they need INDEPENDENT proofs because either leg dropping alone leaks a different column (limit_value vs used_value), so the file has BOTH a "policies-CTE leg isolated" test (orgA has an override, orgB has overrides on different resources, no counters) AND a "usage-CTE leg isolated" test (both tenants have a quota_usage counter on the SAME resource with DISTINCT used_value). NO per-id Get => NO (Code, Hint) existence-leak projection: the cross-tenant probe surfaces as an EMPTY projection (resolver returns ok=false, or len(rows)==0), NOT a typed apierr.NotFound — the empty-slice / ok=false IS the existence-indistinguishability proof. The shared-conflict-tuple fixture for the Upsert-UPDATE bystander test MUST be built via upsertOrganizationPolicy (not seedOrgPolicy raw SQL), because using the repository to build the fixture both proves the partial unique index lets the peer row land at all AND aligns baseline.updated_at timestamps so a wrong-row UPDATE shows up as sub-second drift. The corollary "same-resource-in-two-tenants-both-succeed" test is the load-bearing positive-direction pair — without it, a regression that resolved the conflict by (resource,) alone would have prevented the bystander INSERT, and every byte-identical assertion in the UPDATE-branch test would silently pass against a non-existent row. Helper naming caveat: nullable-column helpers MUST get a per-file suffix (stringPtrEqualQP / fmtStringPtrQP) — BE-0440's project-grant file already defines a stringPtrEqual variant in store_test and same-named helpers collide at compile time. Forward-applicable to any future dual-scope ledger-style table (a hypothetical KindFeatureFlagDefault + KindFeatureFlagOverride pairing, or KindPriceTier defaults + organization overrides, would inherit this template).
ralph/progress.txt:103:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, pure-classifier instance (BE-0405 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_reconciliation_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/reconcile/reconcile_canonical_test.go's new canonical pair TestReconciliationCoversCallSites + TestReconciliationPreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Reconciliation Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: reconciliation tests (BE-0405)" subsection; TWENTY-SEVENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0404 verification-suite mega-pattern. Canonical command `go test -run TestReconciliation ./...`, CI step name `Reconciliation tests`, verify.sh prefix #29 inserted between BE-0404's #28 and the trailing optionals renumbered #29-#33 → #30-#34 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 33 → # 34` and synthetic-fixture `# 32 → # 33` updated to match). Three forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST pure-classifier instance — the chokepoint is `reconcile.Diff(desired, actual) Plan` which is a package-level pure function (no receiver, no I/O, no time, no shared state): (1) `THE CONTENTION BURST SHARES NO INSTANCE — EVERY GOROUTINE CALLS THE PACKAGE-LEVEL Diff DIRECTLY` — unlike BE-0395..BE-0398 (which each shared an instance because the chokepoint had observable mutable state) and unlike BE-0404 (which shares a *BreakGlassService), the reconcile burst has NO shared instance: each goroutine calls `reconcile.Diff(desired, actual)` directly with its own scenario-derived inputs. The contention's value is precisely the assertion that the package itself has no shared state — a regression that smuggled in a cached reason table, a sync.Once mutating a per-action map, or a sync.Pool reused without resetting would surface as a per-iteration mismatch even when the aggregate count matched, because every goroutine knows its own predicted closed-set tag. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a package-level pure function (`pkg.Fn(args)` style): skip the shared-instance pattern entirely; the burst's value is the purity assertion on the package. (2) `DETERMINISTIC-ORDERING SELF-CHECK FIRES BEFORE THE TABLE WALK AND BEFORE THE BURST` — the coverage member's third head-of-test self-check calls `Diff(desired, actual)` TWICE on the same inputs for every scenario and reflect.DeepEquals the resulting plans. A regression that introduced map-iteration ordering into the plan emission (e.g. a hashmap walk instead of the existing `sort.Strings(unionKeys)`) would surface here as a fast targeted failure on the affected scenario row, NOT as a confusing per-iteration burst mismatch many lines later. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint emits an ordered output from unordered inputs: pin determinism at the head of the test as its own self-check, separate from the per-row verdict; the property is structurally different from the closed-set tag and deserves its own seam. (3) `THE PER-ROW REDACTION CANARY IS GATED ON A row.seedValueMarker FLAG SO ONLY ROWS WHOSE MUTATOR ROUTES A VALUE THROUGH DesiredValue TRIGGER THE MARKER-SURVIVAL + MARKER-ABSENCE PREDICATE` — the value-free classification contract says env-var values reach Action ONLY via DesiredValue. The canary therefore seeds RECONCILESECRETMARKER ONLY in env-var-changed / env-var-missing scenarios (via the row's mutator, not a generic pre-mutator pass — important: a generic pass would bleed marker into the env-var-missing row's untouched baseline FOO value and produce an unwanted env_var_changed action, false-positiving the row's "missing" prediction). The predicate asserts (a) the marker survives in Action.DesiredValue of at least one update action, (b) the marker is absent from Action.Type / Kind / Reason / EnvVarKey, all ServiceRef ID fields, all DomainRef fields, AND all UnmanagedResource fields. Non-env-var rows (service_missing, domain_renamed, resource_unmanaged, etc.) leave the flag false and the canary is skipped — they exercise the classifier but route no value through DesiredValue. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose closed-set table covers multiple chokepoint output shapes and only SOME shapes route caller-supplied data through a specific output field: gate the canary per-row (a `row.seedValueMarker bool` flag), seed via the row's mutator (not a generic pass), and enumerate every output field that MUST NOT carry the value — the canary is a per-row contract, not a global one. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:189:- Real-quota concurrent oversubscription integration test (BE-0323 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_quota_concurrent_test.go; structural template for BE-0324..0338 — every other quota-dimension PRD story that needs to prove "parallel requests cannot oversubscribe the quota" against the dimension's owning Service.Create call): one Go test, t.Parallel, against testutil.RequireMigratedDB; wires a REAL *quota.Checker over a REAL *store.QuotaRepository + quota.StaticPlanResolver, and a REAL *store.<Resource>Service + REAL *store.AuditRepository, with the only fakes being the Authorizer and JobEnqueuer (recordingAuthorizer / recordingJobs from projectservice_test.go) which always succeed because their failure-path stories are covered elsewhere. Six load-bearing rules: (1) pre-seed quota_usage with used_value=0 for (org, dimension) BEFORE the race so the SELECT FOR UPDATE inside Checker.LockUsage acquires an existing row rather than racing inserts — the lock contract under test is row-lock serialisation, not row-creation; (2) seed quota_policies as scope_kind='plan_default' (not 'organization' override) so the plan-default fallback path inside QuotaRepository.EffectiveLimit is the one exercised — the org-override path has its own dedicated repository test (TestQuotaRepositoryEffectiveLimitPrefersOrgOverride); (3) launch attempts > limit goroutines (BE-0323 uses 20 attempts / limit 5 — 4:1 ratio gives a comfortable margin against any future pool-size or scheduling change without inflating runtime past ~0.5s); each goroutine generates its OWN canonical resource id via domain.MustNewID + a unique canonical slug fmt.Sprintf("p-%02d", i) so a slug conflict cannot disguise itself as a quota rejection; (4) assert successes == limit AND rejections == attempts - limit exactly, NOT >= / <= — the lock contract guarantees an exact split, and a "successes >= limit / <= attempts" range would silently let a regression through; (5) for EVERY rejection, assert all five ExceededDetail fields (Resource, Limit, Requested=1, Reserved==limit, Current==0) because at the moment the lock yields to a losing transaction every winning reservation is already committed — anything less than Reserved==limit means the lock did not serialise — and assert the diagnostic string never echoes any caller input (the i-indexed slug, display name, request id, or correlation id) so a future logging path cannot turn the error into a content channel; (6) post-race assertions cover three independent surfaces of the same invariant: raw count of projects rows for the org (must equal limit), raw count of active quota_reservations rows for the dimension (must equal limit), AND quotaRepo.SumActiveReservations through Store.Read (must equal limit) — the third surface catches a future regression where the table content diverges from the repository's view. Fake-call assertions pin step order at the unit-of-work level: authz.calls == attempts (every transaction reaches authorization) but jobs.calls == limit (rejected transactions never reach the JobEnqueuer because quota fails first and rolls back the whole tx). The dimension constant comes from the public store.QuotaResource* surface (BE-0323 uses store.QuotaResourceProjects) so a future rename surfaces as a compile error rather than a flaky test. NOT applicable to quota dimensions whose enforcement seam lives outside the *Service.Create unit of work (e.g. cpu_millicores / memory_mb / storage_gb where the dimension is summed across child resources — those need a different shape that exercises the dimension's actual enforcement gate). The CapDeploy concurrent_deployments dimension is the load-bearing precedent for "summed across rows" enforcement (DeploymentService.Create at deployment_service.go:209+691) and would clone this template by swapping the Service + dimension while keeping the six rules intact.
ralph/progress.txt:191:- Race-safe local fakes for concurrent integration tests (BE-0324 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/environment_quota_concurrent_test.go atomicAuthorizer / atomicJobs): the shared recordingAuthorizer / recordingJobs / recordingQuota fakes in projectservice_test.go use plain int counters with no synchronisation, so the BE-0323-pattern 20-goroutine concurrent integration test trips the race detector against a live Postgres (BE-0323's TestProjectServiceCreateRejectsParallelOversubscriptionForProjectsQuota fails under -race for the same reason — a pre-existing issue documented under "Known pre-existing -race-with-DB failures" and owed to a focused triage story). For BE-0325..0338 (and any new concurrent integration test) define LOCAL race-safe fakes in the new test file: type atomicAuthorizer struct{ calls atomic.Int64 } satisfying the Authorizer port with calls.Add(1) on Authorize, and the equivalent atomicJobs for the JobEnqueuer port. Read counts with .Load() after wg.Wait(). Keeps the new test green under -race without touching the shared recording fakes or 80+ unrelated test files; the migration back to shared fakes belongs to the triage story when those are made atomic.
ralph/progress.txt:247:- Quota schema (0006_quota_policy_schema): a closed enum reused across tables is a Postgres DOMAIN over text + CHECK (here quota_resource over 14 dimensions, quota_enforcement_mode over hard/soft/metered/disabled); DROP DOMAIN goes in the down migration after the tables. quota_policies holds one limit per (scope, resource) where a row is EITHER a plan_default (text plan, no plans-table FK yet) OR an organization override — enforced by one table CHECK + two partial unique indexes; the checker resolves "org override else plan default". quota_usage is one counter row per (org, resource); quota_reservations are short-lived (amount>0, status active->committed|released|expired, expires_at, settled_at tied to non-active status by CHECK) and expose UNIQUE (organization_id, id) as a composite-FK target; usage_events is append-only (no updated_at/trigger, signed delta) and links reservations via a tenant-safe composite FK. An optional cross-table link inside a tenant uses a plain composite FK (MATCH SIMPLE) — NOT ON DELETE SET NULL, which would null the NOT NULL organization_id.
ralph/progress.txt:264:- ON CONFLICT against a PARTIAL unique index requires the WHERE predicate in the conflict_target clause (`ON CONFLICT (cols) WHERE <same predicate> DO UPDATE`), otherwise Postgres rejects the upsert because the index is not eligible. quota_policies_org_resource_idx is the canonical case: ON CONFLICT (organization_id, resource) WHERE scope_kind = 'organization' DO UPDATE. The freshly-minted id from newQuotaID is only used on insert; on conflict the existing row's id and created_at are preserved (and updated_at is owned by the per-table trigger).
ralph/progress.txt:346:- Per-subtype quota dimension on the services Create unit of work (BE-0326 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/serviceservice.go ServiceService.Create + internal/controlplane/store/service_quota_applications_concurrent_test.go; structural template for BE-0327 compose_stacks, BE-0328 databases — every per-subtype dimension whose denominator is a SUBSET of the services table filtered by Kind): the create unit of work calls svc.quota.Reserve TWICE in the same *Tx for a service whose kind consumes a per-subtype dimension — first the existing QuotaResourceServices reservation (counts every service), then a conditional `if service.Kind == ServiceKindXxx { Reserve(QuotaResourceXxx) }` AFTER it and BEFORE the services.Insert. Order matters: the services reservation locks first so a tenant who exhausts the services dimension cannot acquire the per-subtype counter at all; the per-subtype reservation locks second so a tenant who exhausts ONLY the per-subtype dimension still gets a typed apierr.QuotaExceeded whose ExceededDetail.Resource names the per-subtype dimension specifically. Both reservations roll back together on any later failure (Insert, Enqueue, audit Append). Test pattern (clone of BE-0325): TWO quota_usage rows + TWO quota_policies seeded (per-subtype TIGHT at 5, services LOOSE at attempts*2 so the per-subtype dimension is the boundary that bites), assert detail.Resource == QuotaResourceXxx, assert BOTH active reservation tables == success count (because every winning Create reserved one of each). A second non-concurrent test exhausts the per-subtype dimension then proves a sibling-kind Create (compose / database for BE-0326) still succeeds — pins the per-subtype boundary as a regression barrier against a future refactor that drops the Kind guard. BE-0326 already lands the kind-guard inside ServiceService.Create at the same line where Reserve(services) returns nil; BE-0327 and BE-0328 each add ONE more conditional Reserve call right below the BE-0326 block (Kind == ServiceKindCompose / ServiceKindDatabase) and ONE more test file cloned from service_quota_applications_concurrent_test.go with the dimension constant + Kind constant + sibling-kind sanity-success cases swapped. NOT applicable to dimensions that span MULTIPLE service kinds with a non-trivial denominator (cpu_millicores / memory_mb / storage_gb — those count a numeric column on every kind and belong to a different per-numeric-resource pattern still to be designed).
ralph/progress.txt:365:- Repository CRUD invariants test file for a **TENANT-SCOPED short-lived claim/ledger table whose ONLY repository mutation surface is an InsertReservation (one-shot active-row INSERT) plus a single resolver read (SumActiveReservations), with NO per-row Update/Delete/Get, NO version column, a settled_consistent CHECK pairing status<>'active' with NON-NULL settled_at, a CHECK amount>0, a composite-FK out (organization_id, job_id) -> provisioning_jobs added by a LATER migration than the row's own migration, and a database-managed bump-only set_updated_at trigger fired BEFORE UPDATE** (BE-0455 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_reservation_repository_invariants_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0453 (quota_usage -- INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING + SELECT FOR UPDATE primitive) and BE-0451 (quota_policies -- INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert) in six load-bearing ways: (a) the row-shape test must anchor BOTH a nullable-pointer JobID (zero on a no-JobID call, *string round-trip on a non-empty-JobID call) AND a nullable-pointer SettledAt (zero on every active-row insert -- there is no public surface that can mint a settled row); (b) the JobID round-trip leg cannot use a synthetic id alone because migration 0008 added a composite FK quota_reservations_job_fk -> provisioning_jobs (organization_id, id), so the test MUST pre-seed a real provisioning_jobs row (`INSERT INTO provisioning_jobs (id, organization_id, job_type, idempotency_key) VALUES (...)`) before InsertReservation -- a regression that dropped the FK or swapped its column order would surface here; (c) the settled_consistent CHECK violation has NO independent client-input surface (settled_at is not in the INSERT column list of InsertReservation), so the test exploits the OTHER direction: passing Status=ReservationStatusCommitted (non-blank, non-'active') sends settled_at=NULL via the default and the CHECK rejects it, proving InsertReservation can ONLY mint ACTIVE rows; (d) the time-filter leg of SumActiveReservations is a SECOND-AXIS exclusion test alongside the status filter -- a row with status='active' AND expires_at<=now must be excluded by the `expires_at > $3` predicate, so the test pre-seeds an active-but-time-expired row and asserts it is excluded WITHOUT mutating its status (a regression that dropped the time predicate would silently sum already-expired rows); (e) the resource-filter leg seeds TWO active reservations under the SAME tenant with DIFFERENT resources and asserts BOTH `SumActiveReservations(org, A, now)` and `SumActiveReservations(org, B, now)` return their own resource's amount (a regression that dropped the resource predicate would return 11 instead of 3 from either call); (f) the duplicate-ID Conflict test uses an explicit caller-supplied ID instead of relying on the qres_ prefix-mint path, because the PK violation is the constraint and a regression that silently re-minted the explicit id would mask the test (the explicit-ID-preservation test PRECEDES the duplicate-ID test and pins the no-remint contract). Forward-applicable to every future short-lived-claim repository whose public surface is a single one-shot active-row INSERT plus a resolver-read (a hypothetical lease-claim table KindResourceLease with active->held|released|expired, or a future job_attempts row whose public surface is a single INSERT per attempt). Test-helper naming caveat at package store_test: every *_test.go file under internal/controlplane/store/ shares the same package, so the type name `quotaReservationTxRollbackSentinel` must be distinct from quotaPolicyTxRollbackSentinel / quotaUsageTxRollbackSentinel / projectTxRollbackSentinel and friends -- grep `TxRollbackSentinel` before adding a new sentinel. Helper REUSE strategy: this file DEFINES its own `runInsertReservationOrFail` / `loadQuotaReservationRowByID` / `countQuotaReservationRowsForOrg` / `rawQuotaReservationRow` (no shared symbols to reuse), and REUSES the existing `insertReservationRow` / `insertSettledReservationRow` helpers defined in quota_repository_test.go for the SumActiveReservations setup legs because those raw-SQL inserts can stamp the schema's required `settled_at = now()` for non-active rows without going through the repository's active-only mint path.
