ralph/progress.txt:32125:- Implemented: internal/controlplane/store/quota_policy_repository_invariants_test.go — repository-level invariants for QuotaRepository's quota_policies write surface (UpsertOrganizationPolicy) and the ListEffectiveLimits resolver. The existing quota_repository_test.go already owned the per-resource EffectiveLimit semantics (PrefersOrgOverride / FallsBackToPlanDefault / NotFound / TenantScoped) and the reservation flows, so this file is additive only — it pins what the existing file does NOT: INSERT row shape (qpol_<token> id, scope_kind='organization', plan IS NULL, organization_id, timestamps), UPDATE branch identity preservation (id and created_at byte-equal, updated_at refreshed), plan-default rows untouched by org override upserts, FK violation -> Conflict on unknown organization_id, negative limit -> Conflict, unknown enforcement_mode -> Conflict, every EnforcementMode constant round-trips, ListEffectiveLimits deterministic alpha order with override-beats-default, ListEffectiveLimits tenant scoping (orgB cannot see orgA's overrides), ListEffectiveLimits plan isolation (a 'pro' default does not bleed into a 'starter' read), ListEffectiveLimits empty result when unconfigured, Store.Write closure rollback proven via a typed sentinel (errors.Is + EffectiveLimit reports unconfigured), and nil-tx guard -> yerr.CodeInternal.
ralph/progress.txt:32141:  - **Repository-level invariants for an EXISTING table whose CRUD surface is already partially covered (BE-0451 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_policy_repository_invariants_test.go)**: quota_policies is NOT a "first story for this table" — migration 0006 already exists, UpsertOrganizationPolicy / EffectiveLimit / ListEffectiveLimits / ListOrganizationUsage already exist in store/quota.go, and internal/controlplane/store/quota_repository_test.go already owns EffectiveLimit's read semantics + reservation flows. The story's "Repository: <table> CRUD and invariants" line therefore reduces to "fill the invariants gap": pin what the existing test file does NOT cover (row-shape of an Upsert INSERT vs UPDATE, byte-equal preservation of id/created_at across UPDATE, sibling-row non-interference for plan defaults, FK/CHECK/DOMAIN -> Conflict mapping, every enum constant round-trips, ListEffectiveLimits tenant + plan isolation, Store.Write closure rollback, nil-tx -> Internal). Before writing anything, grep for `func Test<RepositoryName><MethodName>` to enumerate what is already pinned, and write tests with NEW names that only address the gap. Reuse the test names verbatim only when intentionally re-shaping coverage; never write a same-named test in another file (vet's "redeclared in this block" fires at package store_test).
ralph/progress.txt:32143:  - **quota_policies has no version column**, unlike projects/environments/services/variables. The acceptance-criterion clause "optimistic versioning where applicable" is therefore N/A — there is no If-Match path to test. Don't invent one; document the absence in the file's package-doc comment so a future reader does not look for a missing test (mirrors the BE-0427/BE-0428 user-table convention).
ralph/progress.txt:32144:  - **Direct DB SELECT for row-shape probes** is the right tool when the repository's public reader projections (EffectiveLimit, ListEffectiveLimits) deliberately omit id/created_at/updated_at/scope_kind/plan: an UPDATE-branch identity-preservation test needs to observe those columns, so the test issues `SELECT id, scope_kind, plan, organization_id, resource, limit_value, enforcement_mode, created_at, updated_at FROM quota_policies WHERE scope_kind='organization' AND organization_id=$1 AND resource=$2`. Wrap the load in a typed rawQuotaPolicyRow struct + a `load<Scope>PolicyRow` helper so the call sites read like the persisted-row assertions the variables invariants tests use, without the helper leaking out of the test file.
ralph/progress.txt:32145:  - **ListEffectiveLimits plan isolation** is a distinct invariant from tenant isolation: even within one organization, a plan_default row keyed by plan='pro' must not appear when the caller passes plan='starter'. The single SQL statement's WHERE clause anchors this (`scope_kind='plan_default' AND plan=$2`), but a future regression that drops the plan predicate (e.g., during a "search across all plans" refactor) would slip past a pure tenant-isolation test. The BE-0451 file therefore probes both: a separate orgB test for tenant scoping AND an inlined 'pro' plan default in the deterministic-order test to prove plan scoping. The 'pro' row is asserted absent by row count + per-row resource equality; no extra fixture cost since both probes share the same setup.
ralph/progress.txt:32149:- Implemented: internal/controlplane/store/quota_policy_tenant_isolation_test.go — repository-layer tenant-isolation tests for the quota_policies table. quota_policies is a dual-scope table (plan_default OR organization) whose ONLY mutation surface is UpsertOrganizationPolicy and whose row body is only observable through the typed resolvers (EffectiveLimit / ListEffectiveLimits / ListOrganizationUsage). No per-id Get / Update / Delete surface exists, so the cross-tenant guarantee at this layer is exhaustively the projection invariant on every resolver leg AND the byte-identical-bystander invariant on the upsert. Coverage (9 tests): (1) EffectiveLimit per-resource is tenant-scoped — orgB's override does not appear when orgA queries the same resource; (2) ListEffectiveLimits rendered count is local to the queried tenant (orgA=1, orgB=3) — no count cross; (3) shared-resource isolation (both tenants override `projects` with distinct limit/mode — each sees its OWN); (4) plan isolation (orgA on 'starter' must not see 'pro' plan defaults); (5) ListOrganizationUsage is tenant scoped at BOTH the policies CTE and the usage CTE; (6) shared-counter isolation (both tenants have a quota_usage counter for the same resource with distinct used_value); (7) Upsert INSERT branch on orgA leaves orgB's bystander row byte-identical (the SAME resource on both tenants — the ON CONFLICT predicate is exercised); (8) Upsert UPDATE branch on orgA leaves orgB's bystander row byte-identical (both tenants pre-seeded with an override on the SAME resource — the maximally adversarial fixture for the partial-unique-index ON CONFLICT target); (9) same-resource upserts in two tenants both succeed (positive-direction pair for the bystander proofs — proves the partial unique index is per-tenant, not global).
ralph/progress.txt:32160:  - YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL=... go test ./... full suite — pre-existing failures (TestEnvironmentRepoListByProjectReturnsDeterministicOrdering, TestOrganizationVariableServicePatch{ValueOnlyPreservesIsSecret,IsSecretOnlyPreservesValue,UpdatesValueAndIsSecret}, TestServiceServiceCreateRejectsParallelOversubscriptionForServicesQuota) reproduce at HEAD without this change; confirmed by re-running the same selector with my file untracked. Unrelated to BE-0452.
ralph/progress.txt:32165:  - **Repository tenant-isolation tests for a DUAL-SCOPE table whose ONLY mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert and whose row body is observable EXCLUSIVELY through typed resolvers (no per-id Get/Update/Delete, no traditional ListBy<parent>)** (BE-0452 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_policy_tenant_isolation_test.go). quota_policies is structurally distinct from every prior tenant-isolation template: it has TWO scope_kind values (plan_default with NULL organization_id, and organization with non-NULL), partial unique indexes per scope (`quota_policies_org_resource_idx (organization_id, resource) WHERE scope_kind='organization'`), and NO version column. The byte-identical-bystander anchor therefore reduces to updated_at ALONE (no trigger-bumped version to anchor on), so the trigger-managed updated_at is the SOLE load-bearing column that surfaces a wrong-row UPDATE even when column writes look correct. The 9-test shape diverges from BE-0440 (project_grant) and BE-0436 (api_key_scope) in four load-bearing ways: (a) the tenant-isolation projection is observed through three resolver legs (EffectiveLimit / ListEffectiveLimits / ListOrganizationUsage), NOT through a single ListByParent — each leg has its own WHERE clause and warrants its own tenant-scoping test; (b) a NEW plan-isolation leg is added (orgA on 'starter' must not see 'pro' plan defaults, even though both rows live in the same table with NULL organization_id) — this is the tenant-isolation analogue of BE-0451's per-plan invariant; (c) the byte-identical-bystander proof is observed via a raw-SQL load of the underlying quota_policies row (loadOrganizationPolicyRow, reused from BE-0451's invariants file), because the typed reader projections deliberately omit id/created_at/updated_at/scope_kind/plan — a regression that stamped correct-looking limit_value and enforcement_mode onto orgB's row would otherwise slip past every typed-resolver projection; (d) the ListOrganizationUsage projection has TWO independent tenant predicates (one in the policies CTE on `organization_id`, one in the usage CTE on `organization_id`) — they need INDEPENDENT proofs because either dropping alone would surface a different leak (a missing policies-CTE predicate leaks peer limit_value; a missing usage-CTE predicate leaks peer used_value). Forward-applicable to any future ledger-style table whose physical realization is a dual-scope row body with two partial unique indexes (a hypothetical KindFeatureFlagDefault + KindFeatureFlagOverride pairing would inherit this template).
ralph/progress.txt:32166:  - **No per-id Get surface == no (Code, Hint) existence-leak projection.** The api_key_scope and variables tenant-isolation files include a `Get-cross-tenant-is-indistinguishable-from-unknown-id` test that anchors on (apierr.NotFound.Code, apierr.NotFound.Hint) being byte-identical between a cross-tenant probe and an unknown-id probe — this protects against an existence oracle in the Hint or Message. quota_policies has NO per-id Get, no per-id Update, no Delete — the cross-tenant probe surfaces as an EMPTY projection (resolver returns ok=false, or len(rows)==0), NOT a typed error. The (Code, Hint) projection therefore has NO analogue here; documenting the deliberate absence in the file's package-doc comment keeps a future reader from looking for a missing test. The empty-slice / ok=false projection IS the existence-indistinguishability proof: a probing caller cannot tell from `EffectiveLimit returns false` whether the resource is unconfigured for this tenant or configured for another, and cannot tell from `ListEffectiveLimits returns no row for projects` whether another tenant has a `projects` override.
ralph/progress.txt:32169:  - **ListOrganizationUsage has two CTE legs that each need independent tenant-scoping proof**. The policies CTE selects from quota_policies WHERE `(scope_kind='organization' AND organization_id=$1) OR (scope_kind='plan_default' AND plan=$2)`; the usage CTE selects from quota_usage WHERE `organization_id=$1`. The result is the FULL OUTER JOIN of the two. A regression that dropped the organization_id leg of EITHER CTE leaks a different column: the policies CTE leaks limit_value / enforcement_mode / Scope, the usage CTE leaks used_value. Two independent tests are warranted — one fixture isolates the policies CTE (orgA has an override, orgB has overrides on different resources, no counters on either side — surfaced through LimitValue), the other isolates the usage CTE (both tenants have a counter row on the SAME resource with DISTINCT used_value — surfaced through UsedValue). Collapsing them into one combined test would still pass against a regression that broke only one of the two CTE predicates.
ralph/progress.txt:32247:  - **Repository-layer CRUD-and-lifecycle invariants test file for a TENANT-SCOPED COUNTER table whose ONLY mutation surface is an INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING + SELECT...FOR UPDATE primitive (LockUsage), with NO per-row Update/Delete, NO version column, NO service_id pointer, and a database-managed used_value mutated by an external code path (quota.Checker), set_updated_at trigger fires BEFORE UPDATE only** (BE-0453 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_usage_repository_invariants_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0451 (quota_policies — single-table INSERT...ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE upsert) in four load-bearing ways: (a) the second-call byte-identity proof must anchor updated_at TOO, because ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING is not an UPDATE and the set_updated_at trigger must not fire — quota_policies's UPDATE leg actively REFRESHES updated_at via the trigger, so its byte-identity proof EXCLUDES updated_at; quota_usage's byte-identity proof INCLUDES updated_at as a load-bearing anchor (a regression that switched to ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE with even a no-op SET would silently bump updated_at and a missing anchor would let that slip past); (b) the peer-row proof for quota_usage is a SAME-TENANT-DIFFERENT-RESOURCE check (the UNIQUE (organization_id, resource) constraint isolates rows by resource within a tenant), distinct from quota_policies's cross-scope peer (plan_default vs organization within the SAME quota_policies table); (c) the "row already exists with non-zero value" leg has no quota_policies analog — for quota_usage we pre-seed a row at used_value=17 via direct SQL (seedQuotaUsage from usage_test.go) and prove BOTH the seeded id `qu_seed_projects` AND the seeded used_value survive the LockUsage call, which is the only way to prove the discarded INSERT's minted qusg_ id does not replace the row's id (a regression that switched to ON CONFLICT (...) DO UPDATE SET id=EXCLUDED.id would replace the row's id and reset used_value to 0 — a catastrophic data-loss path the typed reader projections would not catch); (d) the DOMAIN-violation test casts an arbitrary string to store.QuotaResource — `store.QuotaResource("not-a-resource")` — to bypass Go's compile-time check on the typed string and exercise the DOMAIN quota_resource CHECK at the database layer; this is the same casting pattern quota_policies uses for unknown enforcement_mode (`store.EnforcementMode("not-a-mode")`).
ralph/progress.txt:32250:  - Codebase Patterns: ADDED a new top-level pattern entry (this story's quota_usage shape is structurally distinct from BE-0451's quota_policies upsert shape; future ledger-style counter tables with the same INSERT-DO-NOTHING + SELECT-FOR-UPDATE primitive — e.g. quota_reservations is a different shape with status lifecycle, but a future per-tenant-per-resource counter would mirror this exactly — should follow BE-0453, not BE-0451).
ralph/progress.txt:32302:  - **Repository tenant-isolation tests for a TENANT-SCOPED COUNTER table whose ONLY public mutation is INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (organization_id, resource) DO NOTHING + SELECT ... FOR UPDATE (LockUsage), where the row body's used_value is increment-managed BY AN EXTERNAL CODE PATH (quota.Checker, not the repository), with NO per-id Get/Update/Delete surface, NO version column, NO service_id pointer, and the cascade-delete tenant isolation NOT covered by the existing single-tenant cascade test** (BE-0454 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/quota_usage_tenant_isolation_test.go). Structurally distinct from BE-0452 (quota_policies, dual-scope UPSERT) in four load-bearing ways: (a) NO typed-resolver coverage is added here — ListOrganizationUsage's two-CTE-leg tenant scoping is already proved in quota_policy_tenant_isolation_test.go (BE-0452) AND in usage_test.go (TestQuotaRepositoryListOrganizationUsageTenantScoped), and re-asserting would cause same-named tests to collide at compile time across the shared store_test package; the cross-tenant id-leak surface to defend is LockUsage's INSERT/SELECT primitive, which the typed projection cannot expose (the projection omits id by construction) — so the bystander proofs use raw-SQL row loads instead; (b) the bystander invariant has TWO peer classes — same-resource and cross-resource — both anchored on the same baseline tenant in ONE test (test 4), because both classes are governed by the SAME ON CONFLICT predicate (organization_id, resource) and a regression that dropped either column would surface in both; this is structurally simpler than BE-0452's split insert/update bystander tests (quota_policies has TWO upsert branches — DO NOTHING never happens, only DO UPDATE — so insert-on-orgA-doesn't-touch-orgB and update-on-orgA-doesn't-touch-orgB are distinct probes; quota_usage's ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING means orgA's INSERT against orgB's existing row is the ONLY upsert branch worth proving and the test surface collapses to one); (c) a NEW probe is added on top of BE-0452's template: a "per-tenant row-count invariance" test (test 3) that does NOT load any row — only counts rows owned by orgA and orgB before and after — to catch a cross-tenant FK swap that the byte-identity probes would miss (the byte-identity probes only observe rows that still exist; a regression that DELETED orgB's row and INSERTED an orgA-owned replacement at the same id would pass byte-identity if id and used_value coincidentally matched, but would fail this count probe); (d) the cascade-delete tenant-isolation test (test 6) is a NEW class of probe quota_policy_tenant_isolation_test.go did NOT carry because TestQuotaCascadeDeleteOnOrganization (quota_schema_test.go) proves single-tenant cascade only — it never checks that a different tenant's rows survive. quota_usage is the right place to add this probe because the cascade fires at the schema level (FK CASCADE on quota_usage.organization_id) and is per-table; quota_policies's cascade is asymmetric (plan_default rows have NULL organization_id and survive deletes by construction, so a "different tenant's rows survive" probe there would conflate two distinct invariants — the plan-default-survives invariant from quota_schema_test.go AND the organization-bystander-survives invariant — and the asymmetry would obscure the test's purpose). For quota_usage there is no plan_default scope, so the cascade-isolation probe is unambiguous: orgB's rows survive byte-identically (id, used_value, timestamps) when orgA is deleted.
ralph/progress.txt:32304:  - **The "positive-direction pair" anchor (test 5)** is the load-bearing complement of every bystander byte-identity proof in tenant-isolation tests, but for quota_usage it has a different mechanical purpose than for quota_policies: in BE-0452 the positive-direction pair (`SameResourceInTwoTenantsBothSucceed`) anchors the bystander proofs because the partial unique index `quota_policies_org_resource_idx (organization_id, resource) WHERE scope_kind='organization'` is partial AND keyed by both columns — so a regression to a global UNIQUE would have made BOTH the insert-bystander and the update-bystander silently pass against missing rows; in BE-0454 the equivalent positive-direction pair anchors the bystander proofs because `quota_usage_org_resource_idx (organization_id, resource)` is full (not partial — quota_usage has no scope_kind column) but is per-tenant, so the same anchor mechanic applies even though the partial-index angle drops out. Going through the repository surface on BOTH tenants — not via raw INSERTs — is the load-bearing detail: a regression that hard-coded a specific organization_id inside the LockUsage SQL itself would surface here too.
